  "sql-82": `
<h2>Compound predicates: AND, OR, NOT</h2>
<p>The manual builds the <code>WHERE</code> clause step by step: comparisons, <code>LIKE</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IN</code>, and <code>IS NULL</code> can be combined with <code>AND</code> and <code>OR</code>. That matches what you already practiced; this lesson stresses <strong>precedence</strong> and <strong>NOT</strong>.</p>
<h3>Precedence and parentheses</h3>
<p><code>AND</code> binds tighter than <code>OR</code>. Without parentheses, <code>WHERE a = 1 OR b = 2 AND c = 3</code> is read as <code>a = 1 OR (b = 2 AND c = 3)</code>. When in doubt, add parentheses so the intent is obvious to the next reader (and to you in six months).</p>
<h3>NOT</h3>
<p><code>NOT</code> negates the comparison immediately following its rules of precedence. For example, to exclude a pair of jobs in Italy, the book uses <code>AND NOT (JOB = 'S' OR JOB = 'W')</code> — the parentheses group the OR before negation.</p>
<pre>SELECT job, name
FROM persons
WHERE country = 'ITALY'
  AND (job = 'S' OR job = 'W')
ORDER BY job, name;
SELECT job, name
FROM persons
WHERE country = 'ITALY'
  AND NOT (job = 'S' OR job = 'W')
ORDER BY job, name;</pre>
<div class="lesson-callout">
<p>SQLite tip: use <code>IS NOT NULL</code> rather than <code>NOT (col IS NULL)</code> for clarity when filtering nulls.</p>
</div>
<hr class="lesson-divider"/>
<h3>Why this topic shows up in real projects</h3>
<p>Buggy boolean logic in <code>WHERE</code> clauses is a classic source of silent wrong counts — reports look plausible but exclude or double-count rows. Explicit parentheses are cheap insurance.</p>
<hr class="lesson-divider"/>
<h3>Self-hosted study loop (no paywall)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Predict</strong> — for three predicates A, B, C, write two different parenthesis versions and predict different row sets.</li>
<li><strong>Minimum repro</strong> — three-row table, two queries that differ only by parentheses.</li>
<li><strong>Check yourself</strong> — explain aloud why <code>NOT a = 1</code> parses as <code>NOT (a = 1)</code> in SQL.</li>
<li><strong>Break it</strong> — remove parentheses from a compound <code>OR</code>/<code>AND</code> expression and observe the result change.</li>
</ol>
`,
