<section class="lh-sec-study-pack" aria-label="Security+ expanded study notes">
<h2>Security+ prep — expanded study guide</h2>
<p class="lh-sec-lede">This block is generated to mirror <strong>CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701)</strong> study depth: definitions, exam psychology, and workplace application. It supplements the short curriculum bullets above—read both. Lesson <code>lh-sec-l1-04</code> · Level 1 — Foundations (CIA, risk language, safe computing). Sections below rotate per lesson so adjacent topics do not repeat the same blurbs.</p>
<h3>Where this sits in the Security+ landscape</h3>
<p>Expect “which control type?” questions—administrative vs technical vs physical—tied to realistic workplace moments like contractor onboarding.</p>
<h3>How this lesson shows up on the exam</h3>
<p>Security+ rewards <strong>vocabulary precision</strong> and <strong>scenario judgment</strong>. For Level 1 topics, expect paired questions that test whether you know the <em>definition</em>, the <em>correct control family</em>, and the <em>failure mode</em> when misapplied. Use the key terms below as a checklist: if you cannot explain a term in under 20 seconds, revisit the concept.</p>
<ul>
<li>If the question references <strong>contractors</strong>, expect answers about temporary accounts, expiration, and scoped access—not permanent admin.</li>
<li>For <strong>IoT</strong>, default passwords and unsegmented VLANs are common exam villains; change defaults and isolate.</li>
<li>For crypto/TLS items, verify whether the question is about <strong>confidentiality</strong>, <strong>integrity</strong>, <strong>authentication</strong>, or <strong>non-repudiation</strong>—pick the property that actually matches the mechanism described.</li>
<li>Regulatory mentions (HIPAA-style, PCI) usually want <strong>least data</strong>, <strong>encryption</strong>, and <strong>access reviews</strong>—not “collect everything for analytics.”</li>
<li>If data crosses borders, answers may touch <strong>data residency</strong> and lawful access regimes—pick the legally grounded option.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Key terms (memorize and be able to contrast)</h3>
<dl class="lh-sec-dl"><dt><strong>SSO</strong></dt><dd>Single sign-on federates trust to an IdP—misconfigured SAML/OIDC trusts are high-impact; validate metadata and signing keys.</dd><dt><strong>DevSecOps</strong></dt><dd>Embed security gates in CI/CD with fast feedback—policy-as-code, secret scanning, and SBOMs support supply-chain resilience.</dd><dt><strong>Purple teaming</strong></dt><dd>Collaborative attack/defense exercises to validate detections without surprise production harm—scope and safety first.</dd><dt><strong>Full packet capture</strong></dt><dd>High fidelity evidence for investigations—storage, privacy, and legal review constraints apply.</dd><dt><strong>Compensating control</strong></dt><dd>Alternative control that meets intent when primary control cannot be implemented—must provide equivalent or greater assurance with documentation.</dd><dt><strong>Firewall</strong></dt><dd>Enforces allow/deny rules on traffic—stateful inspection tracks sessions; default-deny with explicit permits is the secure baseline.</dd><dt><strong>SIEM</strong></dt><dd>Security information and event management—centralize logs, parse fields, correlate alerts; tuning reduces false positives and fatigue.</dd><dt><strong>Separation of duties (SoD)</strong></dt><dd>No single person can complete a sensitive transaction alone—classic finance control that also limits insider abuse.</dd><dt><strong>RPO / RTO recap</strong></dt><dd>RPO = max acceptable data loss time; RTO = max acceptable outage—drive backup frequency and HA design.</dd><dt><strong>Exploit</strong></dt><dd>A technique or code that takes advantage of a vulnerability to achieve an attacker goal (exfiltration, persistence, disruption).</dd><dt><strong>Zero Trust (headline)</strong></dt><dd>Assume breach; verify explicitly per request using identity, device posture, and least privilege—not “trust the LAN.”</dd><dt><strong>IAM least privilege</strong></dt><dd>Grant minimum API/console rights; prefer roles over long-lived keys; audit risky admin-equivalent paths regularly.</dd><dt><strong>Token replay</strong></dt><dd>Reusing a stolen token/session artifact—rotation, binding, and short TTLs reduce window.</dd><dt><strong>Kill chain / cyber kill chain</strong></dt><dd>Stages of intrusion—mapping controls and detections to stages clarifies coverage gaps.</dd></dl>
<h3>Quick contrast table (recall drill)</h3>
<div class="lh-sec-table-wrap">
<table class="lh-sec-recall">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Property</th>
<th>Typical exam angle</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Confidentiality</strong></td>
<td>Who may read? Encryption, ACLs, DLP, classification.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Integrity</strong></td>
<td>Was it modified? Hashing, signatures, change control, backups.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Availability</strong></td>
<td>Can authorized users work? HA, patching windows, DDoS defense, DR.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Authenticity / non-repudiation</strong></td>
<td>Prove source and intent—signatures, strong issuance, tamper-evident logs.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Fact pack (high-signal trivia)</h3>
<p>Short statements you can verify in official courseware—use them to catch “sounds right” distractors.</p>
<ul>
<li>KMS key policies can deny even root in AWS when configured—explicit deny wins.</li>
<li>NIST CSF functions: Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover—useful mental scaffold for mapping controls.</li>
<li>Sigma rules are vendor-agnostic detection snippets—tuning required per log source fields.</li>
<li>GraphQL batching can amplify complexity attacks—depth and cost limits are first-class controls.</li>
<li>Terraform state can hold secrets—remote secure backend and scanning are baseline hygiene.</li>
<li>FIDO2 security keys resist phishing because the origin is bound cryptographically—unlike TOTP seeds typed into a page.</li>
<li>Immutable backups mitigate ransomware that tries to delete online copies—air-gapped or WORM patterns.</li>
<li>Race conditions in file operations underpin some TOCTOU issues—exam may reference unsafe temp files.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Wrong vs right mental models</h3>
<ul>
<li>Wrong: “Share admin passwords in the vault ‘for emergencies.’” → Right: Break-glass accounts with logging, rotation, and dual control after use.</li>
<li>Wrong: “We have logs, so we are secure.” → Right: Logs without <strong>tuning, retention, and review</strong> are decoration.</li>
<li>Wrong: “NAT is a security feature.” → Right: NAT complicates addressing; it is not a substitute for firewall rules and host controls.</li>
<li>Wrong: “Zero Trust = no firewalls.” → Right: Zero Trust changes <strong>trust assumptions</strong>; network controls remain, differently applied.</li>
<li>Wrong: “Encryption fixes data quality.” → Right: Encryption protects data; it does not validate correctness or authorization.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Standards &amp; frameworks (one-liners)</h3>
<ul>
<li>CIS Controls: prioritized safeguards list—good for baseline gap conversations in interviews.</li>
<li>HIPAA Security Rule: administrative, physical, technical safeguards for PHI—BAA and minimum necessary appear often.</li>
<li>COBIT / ITIL touch governance and service management—sometimes appear as “process maturity” distractors vs technical fixes.</li>
<li>ISO 27005: risk assessment guidance—ties likelihood/impact treatment to management decisions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Interview-style prompts (say aloud)</h3>
<ul>
<li>How does <strong>shared responsibility</strong> change your logging strategy in IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS?</li>
<li>What logs would you want for detecting <strong>lateral movement</strong> in an AD environment at a high level?</li>
<li>How does <strong>DMARC alignment</strong> reduce business email spoofing—what breaks if SPF passes but DKIM fails?</li>
<li>Explain the difference between <strong>risk</strong>, <strong>threat</strong>, and <strong>vulnerability</strong> with one workplace example each.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Deep explanation (read slowly)</h3>
<p><strong>Depth 1 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Security operations value <strong>repeatable evidence</strong>: timestamps, authoritative configuration sources, and tamper-aware logs beat heroic memory after an incident. Many exam questions hinge on whether an action preserves integrity and accountability rather than whether it feels fast.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 2 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Access control stems frequently mix <strong>role</strong> vs <strong>attribute</strong> vs <strong>rule</strong> models; pick the answer that matches the scenario’s constraints (temporary contractor, cross-department data, dynamic tags on resources).</p>
<p><strong>Depth 3 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Mainframe and legacy integration appears as “we cannot patch.” The Security+ answer is usually <strong>compensating controls</strong>, isolation, monitoring, and a documented risk acceptance—not “ignore CVEs.”</p>
<p><strong>Depth 4 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Typosquatting and dependency confusion prey on <strong>human trust in package names</strong>; mitigations include private registries, pinning, and provenance verification.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 5 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Certificate pinning failures during rotation teach why <strong>key continuity strategy</strong> must be planned before mobile releases.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 6 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Social engineering callbacks to <strong>pretexting</strong>, <strong>baiting</strong>, and <strong>vishing</strong>—name the subtype when the stem describes the tactic.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 7 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Vulnerability management is a lifecycle: scanners find candidates, analysts prioritize with exploitability and business context, owners remediate, and verification closes the loop. “Patch everything immediately” is rarely operational; <strong>risk-based SLAs</strong> are the adult answer.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 8 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Business continuity answers should reference <strong>RTO/RPO</strong>, <strong>workarounds</strong>, and <strong>tested restores</strong>; DR without practiced failover is wishful thinking, and exam writers know it.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 9 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Container escape questions hinge on <strong>kernel boundary</strong> and misconfigured capabilities—namespaces are helpful but not a complete trust boundary without seccomp, AppArmor, and least-privileged service accounts.</p>
<p><strong>Depth 10 — lens on <em>L1-04: Privacy vs security</em>:</strong> Evidence bags and write blockers signal <strong>forensics awareness</strong>; touching disks before imaging is a classic wrong answer.</p>
<h3>Controls &amp; engineering habits</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prevent:</strong> least privilege, secure defaults, hardened baselines, input validation, architecture that shrinks trust zones.</li>
<li><strong>Detect:</strong> authoritative logging, correlation, integrity monitoring, tuned detections that reduce alert fatigue.</li>
<li><strong>Respond:</strong> playbooks with legal/HR hooks, containment preserving evidence, comms that avoid tipping attackers.</li>
<li><strong>Recover:</strong> tested restores, disaster runbooks, post-incident metrics that change systems—not blame individuals alone.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Common exam traps</h3>
<ul>
<li>Selecting <strong>security through long passwords only</strong> while ignoring MFA and phishing resistance.</li>
<li>Believing <strong>MAC filtering</strong> is strong authentication for Wi-Fi.</li>
<li>Choosing <strong>anonymous FTP</strong> for “convenience” in any business context stem.</li>
<li>Picking “encrypt everything with AES” when the problem is <strong>broken access control</strong> on the API.</li>
<li>Believing <strong>obscurity</strong> (hidden URLs) is equivalent to <strong>authorization</strong>.</li>
<li>Choosing “disable antivirus” or “turn off firewall” as a troubleshooting first step.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Scenario practice</h3>
<p><strong>Workplace scenario:</strong> Junior staff proposes a quick fix for “L1-04: Privacy vs security” that disables logging on a noisy host. Walk through why that is unacceptable and propose a <strong>tuning</strong> path that preserves <strong>integrity and retention</strong> commitments.</p>
<p><strong>Follow-up drill:</strong> Draft five <strong>yes/no questions</strong> you would ask the system owner to decide whether this is containment-first or preserve-forensics-first.</p>
<p class="msg info"><strong>Lab ethics:</strong> Treat every command as if it were under a written Rules of Engagement. No probing of systems you do not own or lack authorization to test.</p>
</section>
